<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amédée-François Lamy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amédée-François Lamy was a French army officer who led the military escort of the Foureau–Lamy mission across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> in 1898–1900. Joining the other French columns on the Chari, he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kousseri">Kousseri</a> on 22 April 1900; Rabih's army was destroyed and Rabih killed, but Lamy was mortally wounded. The French post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndjamena">Fort-Lamy</a>, now N'Djamena, was named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amedee-francois-lamy.jpg" alt="Amédée-François Lamy" width="205" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CommandantLamy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
